  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain for southeastern areas tonight will \ntransition to numerous showers and isolated thunderstorms across\nmost of the area Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA broad and slow moving upper level trough over the Hudson Bay\nextending south towards the Great Lakes will continue to provide\nunsettled weather and direct waves of subtropical moisture\nnorthward towards Maine. The first wave tonight will be from \nthe merger of the polar and subtropical jets over the Mid-\nAtlantic, which is currently producing a broad area of rain from\nWest Virginia into southern Maine. Precipitation will \noverspread the area this evening, mainly affecting areas along \nand southeast of interstate 95. Precipitation amounts up to a \nhalf inch are possible through Sunday morning over these areas.\n\nMeanwhile, a stronger shortwave trough will be rounding the base of\nthe longwave trough over the northern Great Lakes into western \nQuebec, forcing a stronger area of low pressure to deepen and \ndirect a cold front towards the area on Sunday. This will be \nmore convective and showery compared to the overnight \nprecipitation, and will also be located more over the north in \ngeneral. CAPE is forecast to be limited to a few hundred joules.\nAlthough mid-level lapse rates are not as favorable as when \nsmall hail was observed in stronger convective showers this past\nThursday and Friday, there is still potential for small hail \ndue to the low freezing levels and CAPE/convective air parcels \nreaching the -20C level. Similarly, some of the stronger cells \ncould produce lightning strikes, so isolated thunderstorms are \nforecast across much of the area away from the Downeast marine \nlayer.\n\nPatchy fog is also possible, especially Sunday night to the\nsouth and east of interstate 95 as winds diminish and soil\nmoisture remains elevated from recent rain and ongoing showers.\nMost fog should move to the east by Monday morning, but some\nimpact to the Monday morning commute is possible, especially\nacross coastal Washington county. The continued wet weather\npattern will lead to continued mitigation of the long term\ndrought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions are expected Tuesday and\nWednesday, giving the region a break from frequent rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSlight ridging pattern builds in at the surface, with a mid-\nlevel shortwave moving through on Tuesday. Ridging helps to \nclear out skies, with a potential for some breezy and dry \nconditions during the day. This will be a break from repeated \nrainfall the past week throughout the region, especially with \ngrowing season upon us. Skies become partially cloudy by \nTuesday afternoon. Dry conditions persist through Wednesday, as \nhigh pressure moves east over the region. Daytime highs in the \nlow-to-mid 50s in the north, and mid-to-upper 50s Downeast on \nboth Tuesday and Wednesday. Overnight lows Tuesday in the 30s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for frost over the Downeast region \nTuesday night, which could impact sensitive crops.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nPartially cloudy skies with cool overhead air mass results in a\nchilly Tuesday night, with low temperatures reaching just above\nfreezing, causing a potential for some frost development. Frost\ncan impact any crops recently planted, so plan accordingly to\nprotect agriculture.",
